# What a digest costs, watched.
#
# sha256 is a streaming algorithm: it walks the padded message sixty-four
# bytes at a time, carrying eight state words, and everything a block builds —
# the sixty-four schedule words and the sixty-four rounds — is dead the moment
# the next block starts. So the arena should hold a constant amount however
# long the message is.
#
# It did not, for most of a month. On 2026-08-31 an 8,800-byte message read
# 90,177,536 arena bytes and an 88,000-byte one 889,192,464: nothing was
# reclaimed between blocks. The loops held the eight words as a list, a fresh
# one each round, and a list built in one round and read in the next is a
# value that crosses a rewind. The carry tier that could have copied it out is
# closed to library loops, so the loops kept every block's garbage.
#
# Since 2026-09-23 the words travel as eight arguments, each round passing six
# along and computing two, and the answer becomes a list once, when a block's
# sixty-four rounds are done. Nothing heap-shaped crosses a round or a block,
# both loops rewind, and bench/cost_golden_digest.txt reads one arena block
# where it read two.
#
# The message here is forty-four bytes, one padded block, so this pins the
# constant rather than the slope: what one block costs to digest.
#
# Watched, not frozen: regenerate deliberately and say which way it went.
import "std/sha256"
import "std/text"

message = "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog."

pub play = print (text/slice (sha256/hex (text/bytes message)) 1 16)
